  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1...Greater coverage of showers and thunderstorms is \nexpected from Monday through Tuesday night, some of these storms may \nproduce gusty winds and heavy rainfall.\n\nOverhead today, a broad ridge across much of the western and central \nportions of the CONUS, with a longwave trough centered over the \nNortheastern Seaboard. With this pattern, the central and eastern \nGreat Lakes will be under the ridge-trough transition zone, where a \nshortwave trough will dive southeast across the Great Lakes tonight \ninto Tuesday. Ahead of the trough, diurnal heating along with \ndewpoint temperatures in the 60s will support showers and \nthunderstorms to develop inland from the lake breeze boundaries this \nafternoon. However, unlike this past weekend, there will be better \ncoverage of showers and thunderstorms today due to higher CAPE \nvalues.\n\nWith the passage of the shortwave trough, an associated surface low \nis expected to move across the central Great Lakes and toward Lake \nErie today and through tonight. With this system, there is an area \nof increasing mid-level winds, steepening mid-level lapse rates, and \nhigh PWAT values that will move across the Ohio valley and into \nportions of western New York tonight. Additionally, mean 0-6km wind \nshear will increase to between 25 and 30 knots across far western \nNew York, while remain under 20 knots east of the Genesee Valley. \nThis being said, the risk for an isolated strong thunderstorm \nincreasing across western New York tonight, especially if organized \nconvection develops west of the area. Timing for storm development \nis 05Z to 11Z Tuesday (Midnight to 7AM Tuesday). Primary hazards for \nany storms are damaging winds and locally heavy rainfall.\n\nUpper level troughing will remain overhead the eastern Great Lakes \nTuesday, with the surface low becoming vertically stacked and \nstationary across western and central New York. Diurnal heating \nwithin a moist and soggy environment will support numerous showers \nand thunderstorms. Despite weak winds within the stacked low, a low-\nlevel jet will wrap around the low supporting a few strong \nthunderstorms to develop and result in locally heavy rainfall across \nportions of western and central New York Tuesday afternoon and into \nthe evening. \n\nThe surface low will move into southern New England Tuesday night, \nsupporting the warm moist conveyor belt to move into eastern Lake \nOntario, supporting the potential for showers and thunderstorms \nproducing heavy rainfall to persist. Northerly flow and wrap-around \nmoisture will move into western NY and chances for showers will \nremain overnight. \n\nAll of this being said, there is a Marginal Risk for Severe Weather \ntoday and tonight, and a Marginal Risk for Excessive Rainfall Today \nand Tuesday. \n\nKey Message 3...Chances for showers and thunderstorms are expected \nto linger into Thursday, with drier conditions for the end of the \nwork week.\n\n850mb temperatures will tumble towards +10 to +13 degrees Celsius \nWednesday, as the stacked low will persist across the Northeast. \nPlenty of moisture will persist across the area Wednesday, before \ndrier air builds into the region Wednesday night through Thursday. \nDiurnal heating will support another day of showers and \nthunderstorms across the region. Cooler weather is expected \nWednesday and Thursday with highs in the 70s. \n\nHigh pressure will move into the eastern Great Lakes region for the \nend of the week bringing dry and seasonal weather.",
